  Here is the simplest, most memorable way to understand and explain Creditz tomorrow—using a driving and highway analogy that anyone can instantly grasp.
  ──────
  ### 1. The Big Picture: The 200-Kilometer Highway Journey

  Imagine getting your engineering degree is like a 200-kilometer road trip.

  • The Goal (Destination): You must collect 200 Activity Points (like driving 200 km) across 8 semesters to get your degree clearance certificate.
  • The Speed Limit (Semester Cap): You are not allowed to drive all 200 km in the final semester. There is a strict speed limit of max 30 points per semester. This forces students to stay active continuously
  throughout all 4 years.
  • Different Fuel Types (Category Caps): You cannot drive on just one type of fuel. The college sets limits: max 50 points from Online Courses, max 60 from Hackathons, max 40 from Sports/Volunteering. You
  need a balanced journey.
  ──────
  ### 2. The Three People on the Road

  #### 🚗 1. The Student (The Driver)
  • What they do: Drives the car and collects toll receipts (certificates from hackathons, NPTEL courses, internships, blood donation camps).
  • Their Dashboard:
      • A big Speedometer / Progress Meter showing: "You have 30 out of 200 points. You need 170 more points to graduate."
      • A Semester Fuel Gauge showing how close they are to the 30-point semester limit.
      • A simple button to snap a photo of a certificate and submit it.

  ──────
  #### 👮 2. The Faculty Mentor (The Checkpost Officer)

  • What they do: Every student is assigned a mentor (like an officer at a highway checkpost).
  • Their Job: When a student submits a certificate, the mentor inspects it:
      • Is the certificate genuine?
      • Does the student's name on the certificate match the college roll number?
      • If everything looks good → Click APPROVE (points get credited to the student's meter).
      • If it’s blurry or fake → Click REJECT with a reason ("Please re-upload a clear copy").

  ──────
  #### 🏛️ 3. The HOD (The Highway Transport Commissioner)

  • What they do: The Head of the Department doesn't check every individual certificate. They look at the entire city's traffic from a control room:
      • How many total students are on track to graduate?
      • How many students are stuck in the red zone ("At Risk" of not clearing their degree)?
      • Are mentors reviewing submissions on time, or are there pending backlogs?
      • If needed, they adjust the speed limits (change category caps in the rulebook) or reassign students to different mentors.

  ──────
  ### 3. Where Does Gemini AI Fit In? (The Automated Speed Camera & Scanner)

  In a normal college, mentors have to manually read hundreds of paper certificates, check dates, and type points by hand. It's slow and vulnerable to fake certificates.

  In Creditz, we built an automated AI scanner using Google Gemini 1.5 Flash:

  1. When the student uploads a certificate photo, Gemini acts like an automated highway camera: it instantly reads the document text.
  2. It auto-fills the Course Title, Organization (NPTEL, Coursera, IEEE), and Date so the student doesn't have to type it manually.
  3. It performs an instant fraud/tamper check: It compares the name printed on the certificate against the student's college profile. If a student submits their friend's certificate, Gemini flags it with an
  amber warning: "Suspicious: Name mismatch detected."
  4. When the mentor opens the review screen, they see the certificate on the left and Gemini's AI Audit Card on the right with a confidence score (e.g. 96% Verified). The mentor can approve with one click.
  ──────
